Chapter: ZigPlugRing
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The ring-fed plug body: the same parser and emitter as ZigPlug behind
 the compiler's own serial-ring intake instead of the TCP stack. The
 heap probe (IrmemHarness) measures the TCP receive path at ~130 bytes
 of guest heap per byte of IR before the parser sees anything -- four
 payload materializations per frame plus a per-character text
 conversion, none of it restored, against a bump allocator that never
 frees. read-serial-cce is a machine-code loop that builds the Text at
 one byte per byte and stops only at NUL, which no IR-CCE byte ever is
 (code 0 is reserved). That difference is what admits IRs past the TCP
 intake ceiling.

 The output side is the zig text as the emitter streams it, then a
 `RINGPLUG-END` line; guest.py's capture ends there. See the note below
 the body for why there is no longer a `SIZE:` line.

Section: Body

  opening : [Console, FileSystem] Nothing = act
   mode-result <- read-line
   when mode-result
    is Just (mode) -> ring-plug-emit mode
    is None -> print-line-uni "RINGPLUG-FAIL: no mode line"
  end

  ring-plug-emit : Text -> [Console, FileSystem] Nothing
  ring-plug-emit (mode) = act
   src <- read-serial-cce mode
   let parsed = parse-ir-chapter src
   in act
    emit-zig-chapter-stream (parsed.chapter) (parsed.type-defs)
    print-line-uni ""
    print-line-uni ("RINGPLUG-END ir=" & show (text-length src) & " defs=" & show (list-length ((parsed.chapter).defs)))
   end
  end

 SINCE U63 THE EMITTER STREAMS. Upstream replaced `emit-zig-chapter`, which
 answered the whole reply as one Text, with `emit-zig-chapter-stream`, which
 prints each definition above a heap mark and restores it: the reply is never
 held whole, so its length is not known before the first byte, and there is
 no `SIZE:` to announce. The capture ends at the `RINGPLUG-END` line instead
 (guest.py's sentinel mode), and the stream is printed with `print-uni`,
 so it arrives as text rather than as raw CCE.
